SAPL Node - Getting Started
===========================

This directory (/var/lib/sapl) is where the PDP looks for
.saplbundle files. The node is configured in BUNDLES mode with
signature verification enabled by default.

The node will not start until bundle security, TLS and an
authentication mode are configured. You must provide a signing key
(or explicitly allow unsigned bundles), a certificate (or explicitly
allow plaintext), and credentials (or explicitly allow anonymous
access) before the service can run.


Quick Start (signed bundles)
----------------------------

All commands that read or write /var/lib/sapl/ or /etc/sapl/
require sudo. The data directory is only accessible to the sapl
service account to protect policy confidentiality.

1. Generate a signing keypair (one-time):

   sudo sapl bundle keygen -o /etc/sapl/signing

   This creates signing.pem (private key) and signing.pub (public key).
   Store the private key in your CI secrets or a vault. It is only
   needed for signing bundles, not at runtime. The server only needs
   the public key.

2. Build and sign a bundle from the example policies:

   sudo sapl bundle create -i /var/lib/sapl/example -o /var/lib/sapl/default.saplbundle -k /etc/sapl/signing.pem

3. Configure the public key in /etc/sapl/application.yml.
   Uncomment both the "bundle-security:" line AND the
   "public-key-path:" line beneath it:

   io.sapl.pdp.embedded:
     bundle-security:
       public-key-path: /etc/sapl/signing.pub

4. Configure TLS in /etc/sapl/application.yml. Both APIs serve one
   certificate from PEM files: the certificate followed by its chain,
   and the private key. The files must be readable by the sapl user:

   sudo chown root:sapl /etc/sapl/tls/server.pem /etc/sapl/tls/server.key
   sudo chmod 640 /etc/sapl/tls/server.key

   io.sapl.node:
     tls:
       certificate-chain: /etc/sapl/tls/server.pem
       private-key: /etc/sapl/tls/server.key

   A renewed certificate is served without a restart. The log reports
   a certificate from 14 days before it expires.

5. Configure authentication. Generate an API key and add the printed
   user entry under io.sapl.node.users in /etc/sapl/application.yml:

   sapl generate apikey --id my-client

   io.sapl.node:
     allow-api-key-auth: true

6. Restart the service:

   sudo systemctl restart sapl

7. Verify:

   curl -s --cacert /etc/sapl/tls/ca.pem https://localhost:8080/actuator/health | jq .

   You should see "status": "UP".

The PDP watches this directory for changes. When you deploy a new
bundle, the PDP reloads automatically without a restart.


Writing Your Own Policies
-------------------------

Replace the example with your own policies:

1. Create a directory with your .sapl files and a pdp.json.
   Example:

   {
     "algorithm": {
       "votingMode": "PRIORITY_DENY",
       "defaultDecision": "DENY",
       "errorHandling": "PROPAGATE"
     }
   }

2. Build and sign: sapl bundle create -i ./my-policies -o /var/lib/sapl/default.saplbundle -k /etc/sapl/signing.pem
   The configuration id identifying this publication is recorded in
   the bundle manifest and printed on success. Set it explicitly with
   --configuration-id, or a content-derived id is used.
3. The PDP reloads automatically

See /var/lib/sapl/example/ for a starting point.


Disabling Signature Verification
---------------------------------

For development or evaluation, you can opt out of signature
verification. Add to /etc/sapl/application.yml:

   io.sapl.pdp.embedded:
     bundle-security:
       allow-unsigned: true

The node logs a warning on every startup when signature verification
is disabled. Do not use this setting in production.


Plaintext and Anonymous Access
------------------------------

Where TLS is terminated upstream, by a proxy or a service mesh, or
where an authenticating proxy owns the trust boundary, allow the
insecure mode explicitly in /etc/sapl/application.yml:

   io.sapl.node:
     allow-no-tls: true
     allow-no-auth: true

The rule holds on every address, localhost included. The node logs a
warning on every startup for each opt-in, naming where it was set.


